##### 0. Introduction
This chapter describes motion of an object in 1D. First it introduces the concepts of *change* and its *measurement* by *derivatives*. As a physical application Walck proposes a highly *idealized* car which is restricted to move only in 1D. 

We think that a better *prototypical* system is the more standard *simple harmonic oscillator* constrained to movements in 1D (called: [*simple harmonic motion* (SHM)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Simple_harmonic_motion)). Despite its simplicity there are physical and technical systems working according to the SHM principle. As an example we refer to the [*Scotch Yoke System (SYS)*](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scotch_yoke). The SYS transforms *circular* in to *linear* motion and works e.g. in all sorts of pumps.

Walck provides only Haskell code for some basic concept definitions. We transpiled this into Julia and added some functions so that we could test the definitions with simple numerical examples. To this end we exploit Julia's *multiple dispatch* and introduce a further *method* for the *derivative function* $D$ which accepts as input an *array* of function values. This way it is possible to compute *higher-order derivatives* .

Haskell-functions are *curryfied* and Julia's usually are *not*. As a consequence our type definitions are slightly different from Walck's.

"

